    {
      "id": 1,
      "claim": "A hat made of ordinary kitchen‑grade tin (aluminum) foil is only a few micrometres thick and is not a complete conductive enclosure.",
      "evidence": "Aluminum foil thickness ≈ 0.016 mm; effective electromagnetic shielding requires thickness comparable to skin depth at the frequency of interest and a closed Faraday cage.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Effective shielding of radio‑frequency (RF) signals requires a continuous conductive surface with minimal gaps; a hat leaves large openings (top, sides, back).",
      "evidence": "RF shielding theory: gaps larger than a few percent of the wavelength allow wave penetration; a hat does not meet this criterion.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because the foil is thin and the hat is not a closed cage, it does not appreciably attenuate the broad spectrum of radio waves that carry ordinary communications or any hypothesised “mind‑control” signals.",
      "evidence": "Calculations of skin depth for typical RF (e.g., 100 MHz) show aluminum foil reduces power by only a few dB; gaps dominate loss.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "There is no scientifically verified “mind‑control” signal that could be blocked by any material, let alone a thin hat.",
      "evidence": "Psychology and neuroscience literature contain no evidence of external signals that can directly control thoughts.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Consequently, none of the specific claims in options B–G (blocking alien signals, blocking all radio waves, blocking mind‑control, preventing government control, hiding thoughts, hearing aliens) are supported by physics or evidence.",
      "evidence": "Steps 3 and 4 show the hat provides no meaningful shielding and the alleged signals do not exist.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "The only statement that matches the scientific assessment is that nothing special happens when you wear a tin‑foil hat.",
      "evidence": "Option A states exactly this, and all other options are contradicted by the previous steps.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [5]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "A",
  "confidence": 0.97,
  "considered": [
